  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 AM EDT Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nAs a weak shortwave continues to bring scattered light rain showers \nto the area this morning, expect the showers to dissipate across the \narea by late this morning as the aforementioned shortwave leaves the \narea. With remnant ridging overhead today, expect the highs to \nincrease into the mid 60s over the interior areas by this afternoon \neven despite mostly cloudy skies remaining overhead. Overall, after \nthis morning, expect mainly quiet weather today into Monday as upper \nlevel ridging persists, allowing highs to return into the 60s on \nMonday as well. While we could see some patchy fog late \ntonight/early Monday morning over portions of Upper Michigan, \nconfidence on that is shaky; continue to keep up-to-date with the \nforecast to see the latest fog updates for tonight/Monday morning.\n\nA stacked low of around 996mb over the Plains on Monday brings a \nwarm front into Upper Michigan late Monday that stalls out overhead \nMonday night into the mid-week. With guidance continuing to slow \ndown the progression of the warm front, rainfall amounts continue to \nincrease amongst the differing ensemble and deterministic guidances; \nthe latest NBM has 1+\" of rainfall chances between 70 and 90% across \nthe area from Monday to Wednesday morning, and greater than 2\" \nchances between 10 and 30%. WPC is also highlighting the high \nrainfall amounts and has put the U.P. under a Marginal Risk for \nexcessive rainfall Monday and Monday night. While the soaking \nrainfall will certainly be welcome across the area, currently \nthinking no flooding will be seen due to the ongoing drought \nconditions across Upper Michigan and mainly synoptic-scale forcing \ncreating the ascent (not much instability to create convection). \nSpeaking of which, while the instability would be laughable when \ncompared to the summertime (<100 J/kg), there may be enough to \ncreate a rumble of thunder or two as the rain moves through the area \nas we move from the early to mid portions of the work week given the \nrobust frontogenetic forcing. \n\nMoving into the latter half of the forecast period, confidence is \nslowly beginning to increase on precipitation chances continuing \nthrough the mid to late week period as the latest European and UKMET \nsolutions continue to slow the progression of the low down over the \nUpper Great Lakes. Because of this, chances for wrap-around moisture \nfrom the low are increasing, suggesting that the cold front may \navoid our area altogether as the low's center moves through either \nthe Straits of Mackinac or Lower Michigan Thursday/Friday. However, \nCAA from behind the low could help induce lake enhanced and \neventually lake effect precipitation over the north to northwest \nwind belts late this week, with a mix over to snow possible late \nThursday/early Friday. While there still are some solutions that \nsuggest a more progressive pattern, with the latest 00z run of the \nGFS coming much more in line with the Euro and UKMET, confidence on \nthe slower solution and thus the longer, more continuous rainfall \nacross the area continues to likewise increase.",
